#!/usr/bin/env python3
# aegis-summary: Captures and restores the tenants' DATA
# aegis-group:   backup
# aegis-subcommands: backup list restore
"""aegis-data — captures and restores the tenants' DATA.

It is the other half of `aegis state backup`. That one backs up the
platform's STATE —the store's secrets, the init's trail, the edge's
tfstate—; this one backs up what the tenants GENERATED: their databases
and their objects. Neither half is any good alone: with the state you
raise an empty platform, with the data you have a dump nobody knows where
to put.

    aegis data backup                    # one bundle PER ORGANIZATION
    aegis data list <bundle.age>         # what is inside, without restoring
    aegis data restore <bundle.age> --org ejemplo [--force]

── ONE BUNDLE PER ORGANIZATION, IN ITS OWN FOLDER ──────────────────

    $AEGIS_BACKUPS/
      .aegis-destino                       <- the disk's mark (see below)
      platform/
        aegis-state-<ts>.age              <- written by aegis state backup
      org-ejemplo/
        aegis-datos-org-ejemplo-<ts>.age
      org-portafolio/
        aegis-datos-org-portafolio-<ts>.age

Until 2026-08-11 it was ONE bundle with everything inside. Separating it
per organization changes three things that matter:

  · restoring one does not force you to open everybody else's
    safekeeping — and that safekeeping carries third parties' data;
  · decommissioning an organization is deleting its folder, not editing a
    shared file;
  · and the one that really weighs: the honest question stops being "is
    there a backup?" and becomes "does EVERY organization have a backup?".

That last one is the underlying reason. With a single bundle, a new
organization that was never backed up was INVISIBLE: the file existed, it
had a recent date, and it was not inside. Now the absence has the shape
of a missing folder, and `aegis check` enumerates it against the
contracts.

── THE DISK'S MARK (.aegis-destino) ────────────────────────────────

The typical destination is a second disk, and on a desktop that disk is
usually mounted by the graphical environment at login — it is not in
fstab and it is NOT mounted during boot. If the backup runs in that gap,
the path exists all the same (it is an empty directory on the root disk)
and the bundle is written there: success reported, zero protection, and
the copy that was supposed to survive the disk lives on the disk.

That is why the first capture notes the destination filesystem's UUID
down in `<root>/.aegis-destino`, and the following ones verify it. If it
does not match —it is not mounted, or it is another disk— NOTHING is
written. The UUID is compared and not the mount point because the mount
point is exactly what lies in this failure.

── WHY IT RUNS ON THE OPERATOR'S SIDE AND NOT INSIDE THE CLUSTER ───

It is not a preference: it is the only thing the platform allows without
loosening it. Measured on 2026-08-09:

  1. The namespaces with data are ALL `restricted` (org-*, garage-system,
     ai-system). A Job there CANNOT mount a hostPath, so it cannot write
     into the backups directory.
  2. The egress of a tenant namespace is closed except for DNS. A pod in
     there can dump the database —`allow-intra-namespace` allows it— but
     it cannot GET the dump OUT over the network.
  3. Garage is distroless: no shell. Its backup goes through the S3 API,
     not through the filesystem.
  4. /var/lib/rancher/k3s/storage is not readable without root, and its
     paths carry the PVC's UUID — which on a new instance is ANOTHER one.
     That is, restoring cannot be "copy the directory back" either.

The alternative —a CronJob inside— demands a lax namespace, one network
rule per tenant and a permanent identity able to read every database.
That is a target, and it is a lot of machinery for a one-node platform.
Here the transport is `kubectl exec` (verified without loss: 5136 bytes
inside, 5136 on the host) and `kubectl port-forward` for S3, which
besides dodges the NetworkPolicy legitimately because the traffic is born
on the node.

For the SCHEDULED mode, this is fired from a host timer. It is more
honest than a CronJob: the one that backs up does not live inside what it
backs up.

── WHAT IS CAPTURED AND HOW, WHICH IS WHERE THE CORRECTNESS IS ─────

postgres  `pg_dumpall`, NOT `pg_dump`. Measured: the ROLES live outside
          any database, and a normal `pg_dump` loses them. Restoring
          without them gives a database with no owners and an app that
          cannot get in. `pg_dumpall` also brings the OTHER databases
          (there is a `postgres` one besides the contract's).

bucket    Listing and download over S3 with a SigV4 signature. The object
          travels with its key and its sha256; on restoring, the sha256
          is re-verified before writing and the size after.

THE PASSWORD IS NOT COPIED INTO THE BUNDLE, its FINGERPRINT is stored.
The globals dump already brings each role's SCRAM hash, so the restored
database accepts the password THAT WAS THERE AT CAPTURE TIME. That is
right on the machine that took the capture and wrong on any other one:
the new instance generated its own credential, the dump knows nothing
about it, and the database ends up expecting one password while the pod
presents another. The manifest therefore stores the sha256 of the one
that was there —`restore` COMPARES, and a difference is worth a person,
because restoring a machine is not the same operation as moving an
organization into a new one— and once the dump is loaded, `restore`
REALIGNS the role with the live Secret and proves that the credential
opens the database. Copying the secret into the bundle would have hidden
the discrepancy by creating it in silence; storing the fingerprint
detects it, and realigning closes it.
"""
import argparse
import base64
import datetime
import hashlib
import hmac
import json
import os
import shutil
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import tarfile
import tempfile
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET

try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    sys.exit("pyyaml is missing (python3-yaml)")

# ── canonical python preamble (V-102) ────────────────────────────────
# The product comes out of the variable (bin/aegis exports it) or out of
# this file; the INSTANCE is decided by lib/aegis/paths.py and nobody
# else.
_AEGIS_ROOT = os.environ.get("AEGIS_ROOT") or os.path.dirname(
    os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(_AEGIS_ROOT, "lib"))
from aegis import cli, paths             # noqa: E402

# PLATFORM_DIR is the INSTANCE (the live checkout of the platform), not
# the product. In v2 this line was `dirname(dirname(__file__))` and it
# worked because the command lived INSIDE the instance. On moving the
# code to the product the same line went on compiling and started
# pointing at the wrong place — C1/C2's «dependency invisible to a grep».
PLATFORM_DIR = str(paths.platform_dir())
ORGS_DIR = os.path.join(PLATFORM_DIR, "orgs")
SERVICES = os.path.join(PLATFORM_DIR, "services.yaml")
DEFAULT_DEST = os.environ.get(
    "AEGIS_BACKUPS", os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "aegis-backups"))

red = "\033[31m"; green = "\033[32m"; yellow = "\033[33m"; gray = "\033[90m"; reset = "\033[0m"
if not sys.stdout.isatty():
    red = green = yellow = gray = reset = ""


def die(msg):
    print(f"{red}✗{reset} {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(1)


# ── the destination: that it be the disk we believe ─────────────────
def uuid_of(path):
    """UUID of the filesystem where `path` lives (or None if it cannot be read)."""
    r = subprocess.run(["findmnt", "-no", "UUID", "--target", path],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    return r.stdout.strip() or None


def prepare_root(root):
    """Creates the backups root and verifies that it is the usual disk.

    The failure this prevents is not theoretical and does not announce
    itself: a second disk mounted by the desktop is not mounted during
    boot, the path goes on existing as an empty directory of the root
    disk, and the backup lands there believing it went out. It is
    discovered the day it is needed.
    """
    root = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(root))
    os.makedirs(root, exist_ok=True)
    # `.aegis-destino` and the `uuid` field are the on-disk FORMAT:
    # aegis-check reads this same file to tell whether the disk is the
    # one that was noted down (it reads `uuid` and nothing else).
    # Renaming THOSE TWO is a coordinated change between the two
    # commands and every disk already in use, not a translation.
    # The other two fields are for whoever opens the file with an
    # editor the day something does not add up, so they are prose and
    # they are written in the same language as the rest of the product.
    mark = os.path.join(root, ".aegis-destino")
    current = uuid_of(root)
    if current is None:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} I could not read the UUID of the filesystem of {root} "
              f"(findmnt?): I go on, but WITHOUT the disk guard")
        return root
    if os.path.exists(mark):
        try:
            prev = json.load(open(mark, encoding="utf-8"))
        except Exception:
            prev = {}
        noted = prev.get("uuid")
        if noted and noted != current:
            die(
                f"the backups destination changed DISK and nothing is written.\n"
                f"      noted down : {noted}\n"
                f"      now        : {current}\n"
                f"      {root}\n"
                f"      The usual thing is that the backups disk is not mounted and\n"
                f"      this path is an empty directory of the root disk — that is, the\n"
                f"      safekeeping would fall on the same disk it has to survive.\n"
                f"      Mount it and repeat. If the change is on purpose, delete\n"
                f"      {mark} and the next capture notes it down again.")
    else:
        json.dump({
            "uuid": current,
            "noted_at": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                        .strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
            "why": "if this UUID does not match, the backups disk is not "
                   "mounted and the bundle would land on the very disk it "
                   "has to survive",
        }, open(mark, "w", encoding="utf-8"), indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
        ok(f"destination noted down: {root} (fs {current})")
    if uuid_of("/") == current:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} the destination is on the SAME filesystem as / — a "
              f"failure of that disk takes the data and its backups with it")
    return root


def step(msg):
    print(f"  {gray}·{reset} {msg}")


def ok(msg):
    print(f"  {green}✓{reset} {msg}")


def kubectl(args, binary=False, stdin_data=None):
    r = subprocess.run(["kubectl"] + args, capture_output=True,
                       input=stdin_data, **({} if binary else {"text": True}))
    return r


def secret_value(ns, secret, key):
    """One key of a Secret, in bytes. It is used, never printed.

    There is ONE door and it returns bytes: whoever calls it decides what
    to do with them, and nobody has the excuse of dumping the whole
    object with `-o yaml` to see what is inside. It exists because the
    restore needs the credential OF THE INSTANCE IT IS RESTORING INTO —
    the bundle deliberately carries none.
    """
    r = kubectl(["get", "secret", secret, "-n", ns, "-o",
                 "jsonpath={.data." + key + "}"])
    if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip():
        return None
    return base64.b64decode(r.stdout.strip())


def shq(value):
    """A shell argument, quoted.

    Nothing that goes through here is a secret —a secret does not travel
    in argv, see `realign_role`— but a role and a database name come out
    of a contract, and a contract is text a person writes.
    """
    return "'" + value.replace("'", "'\\''") + "'"


def sql_identifier(name):
    return '"' + name.replace('"', '""') + '"'


def sql_literal(value):
    """A SQL string literal.

    With `standard_conforming_strings` —on by default since 9.1, and the
    engine here is 17— the backslash is an ordinary character, so
    doubling the quote is the whole escape.
    """
    return "'" + value.replace("'", "''") + "'"


# ── discovery ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The same as aegis registry and for the same reason: the list is NOT
# written. It comes out of the contracts and is CONTRASTED against the
# cluster. A source that is on one side and not on the other is exactly
# the condition that makes a backup dangerous —capturing too little
# without finding out— so it is an error and not a warning.
def sources():
    databases, buckets = [], []
    for f in sorted(os.listdir(ORGS_DIR)):
        if not f.endswith(".yaml"):
            continue
        d = yaml.safe_load(open(os.path.join(ORGS_DIR, f), encoding="utf-8"))
        org = d.get("organizacion") or d.get("nombre")
        ns = f"org-{org}"
        for s in d.get("servicios", []) or []:
            if s.get("tipo") == "postgres":
                databases.append({"org": org, "ns": ns, "servicio": s["nombre"],
                                  "pod": f"{s['nombre']}-0",
                                  "secreto": f"{s['nombre']}-credenciales"})
        if (d.get("almacenamiento") or {}).get("bucket"):
            buckets.append({"org": org, "ns": ns, "bucket": org})

    # contrast against the cluster
    r = kubectl(["get", "statefulset", "-A", "-l", "aegis.dev/component=datos",
                 "--no-headers", "-o",
                 "custom-columns=NS:.metadata.namespace,N:.metadata.name"])
    if r.returncode != 0:
        die("I could not read the cluster's StatefulSets (kubeconfig?)")
    alive = {tuple(l.split()) for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()}
    declared = {(b["ns"], b["servicio"]) for b in databases}
    if alive != declared:
        for x in sorted(alive - declared):
            print(f"  {red}!{reset} {x[0]}/{x[1]} lives in the CLUSTER and does not come out of any "
                  f"contract — it would be left WITHOUT a backup")
        for x in sorted(declared - alive):
            print(f"  {red}!{reset} {x[0]}/{x[1]} comes out of a contract and is not in the "
                  f"cluster — either it did not sync, or the contract lies")
        die("the contracts and the cluster do not match: nothing is backed up blind")
    return databases, buckets


# ── SigV4 signature, minimal and without dependencies ───────────────
def _sign(key, msg):
    return hmac.new(key, msg.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()


def s3(method, host, path, query, ak, sk, region="garage", body=b""):
    t = datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
    amz = t.strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
    day = t.strftime("%Y%m%d")
    hpay = hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()
    qs = "&".join(f"{urllib.parse.quote(k, safe='')}={urllib.parse.quote(v, safe='')}"
                  for k, v in sorted(query.items()))
    cr = (f"{method}\n{urllib.parse.quote(path)}\n{qs}\n"
          f"host:{host}\nx-amz-content-sha256:{hpay}\nx-amz-date:{amz}\n\n"
          f"host;x-amz-content-sha256;x-amz-date\n{hpay}")
    scope = f"{day}/{region}/s3/aws4_request"
    sts = (f"AWS4-HMAC-SHA256\n{amz}\n{scope}\n"
           f"{hashlib.sha256(cr.encode()).hexdigest()}")
    k = _sign(_sign(_sign(_sign(("AWS4" + sk).encode(), day), region), "s3"), "aws4_request")
    signature = hmac.new(k, sts.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()
    headers = {
        "Authorization": (f"AWS4-HMAC-SHA256 Credential={ak}/{scope}, "
                          f"SignedHeaders=host;x-amz-content-sha256;x-amz-date, "
                          f"Signature={signature}"),
        "x-amz-content-sha256": hpay, "x-amz-date": amz, "Host": host,
    }
    url = f"http://{host}{urllib.parse.quote(path)}" + (f"?{qs}" if qs else "")
    req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=body or None, headers=headers, method=method)
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=30) as r:
            return r.status, r.read(), dict(r.headers)
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        return e.code, e.read(), dict(e.headers)


def s3_credential(ns):
    """The S3 pair of a namespace, out of its live `garage` Secret.

    Capture reads it to take the objects out; restore reads it to put
    them back — and on restore it is decisive that it be THIS instance's
    and not the bundle's: a bundle carries objects, never keys, so
    restoring on a new machine has to sign with the pair that machine
    generated.
    """
    ak = secret_value(ns, "garage", "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID")
    sk = secret_value(ns, "garage", "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY")
    if ak is None or sk is None:
        die(f"no S3 credential in {ns}/garage")
    return ak.decode().strip(), sk.decode().strip()


def bucket_listing(host, bucket, ak, sk):
    """Every object in a bucket: its key and its size, paging included.

    ONE lister for the three that need it —capture, restore and the
    measurement—, because the paging of ListObjectsV2 is exactly the kind
    of detail that gets written well once and badly the other two times:
    forgetting the continuation token does not fail, it returns the first
    thousand objects and says nothing.

    The size comes out of the listing itself, which is why asking how much
    a bucket weighs costs one request per thousand objects and not one
    request per object.
    """
    objects, token, go_on = [], None, True
    while go_on:
        q = {"list-type": "2"}
        if token:
            q["continuation-token"] = token
        st, body, _ = s3("GET", host, f"/{bucket}", q, ak, sk)
        if st != 200:
            die(f"listing {bucket} gave HTTP {st}: {body[:200]!r}")
        root = ET.fromstring(body)
        nsx = {"s3": root.tag.split("}")[0].strip("{")} if "}" in root.tag else {}

        def h(n, p_):
            return n.find(f"s3:{p_}", nsx) if nsx else n.find(p_)

        for c in (root.findall("s3:Contents", nsx) if nsx else root.findall("Contents")):
            size = h(c, "Size")
            objects.append({"clave": h(c, "Key").text,
                            "bytes": int(size.text) if size is not None else -1})
        tr = h(root, "IsTruncated")
        go_on = tr is not None and tr.text == "true"
        nt = h(root, "NextContinuationToken")
        token = nt.text if nt is not None else None
    return objects


class Tunnel:
    """port-forward to Garage's S3.

    It is needed because 3900 only accepts traffic from tenant
    namespaces, and the operator is not one. The port-forward does not
    violate that rule: the traffic is born on the node, which is where the
    one that backs up lives.
    """

    def __init__(self, ns, svc, port):
        self.ns, self.svc, self.port = ns, svc, port
        self.p = None
        self.local = None

    def __enter__(self):
        s = socket.socket(); s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0))
        self.local = s.getsockname()[1]; s.close()
        self.p = subprocess.Popen(
            ["kubectl", "port-forward", "-n", self.ns, f"svc/{self.svc}",
             f"{self.local}:{self.port}"],
            stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        for _ in range(50):
            try:
                c = socket.create_connection(("127.0.0.1", self.local), 0.3); c.close()
                return self
            except OSError:
                time.sleep(0.2)
        self.__exit__(None, None, None)
        die(f"the port-forward to {self.ns}/{self.svc}:{self.port} did not come up")

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        if self.p:
            self.p.terminate()
            try:
                self.p.wait(timeout=5)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                self.p.kill()

    @property
    def host(self):
        return f"127.0.0.1:{self.local}"


# ── capture ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
def credential_fingerprint(ns, secret):
    """sha256 of the live password, WITHOUT bringing it into the bundle."""
    v = secret_value(ns, secret, "password")
    return hashlib.sha256(v).hexdigest() if v is not None else None


def psql(ns, pod, database, sql):
    """A one-line query against a database, returns stdout."""
    r = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 f'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d {database} -Atc {json.dumps(sql)}'])
    return (r.stdout or "").strip(), r.returncode


def inventory(ns, pod, database):
    """Tables and rows of a database. It is against this that the restore is verified.

    It is not enough for psql to finish without shouting: the drill of
    2026-08-09 left the database DELETED and the command reported
    success. A restore has to MEASURE what it achieved, not trust its exit
    code.
    """
    out, rc = psql(ns, pod, database,
                   "SELECT schemaname||'.'||relname FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY 1")
    if rc != 0:
        return None
    tables = []
    for t in [x for x in out.splitlines() if x.strip()]:
        n, rc2 = psql(ns, pod, database, f'SELECT count(*) FROM {t}')
        tables.append({"tabla": t, "filas": int(n) if rc2 == 0 and n.isdigit() else -1})
    return tables


def databases_of(ns, pod):
    out, _ = psql(ns, pod, "postgres",
                  "SELECT datname FROM pg_database WHERE NOT datistemplate "
                  "AND datallowconn ORDER BY 1")
    return [x for x in out.splitlines() if x.strip()]


def capture_database(b, target):
    """Globals apart, and one database per file. The whole pg_dumpall is NOT used.

    Both reasons came out of the drill, and the second nearly broke the
    organization for real:

    1. Without `--clean`, restoring over a database with data DUPLICATES
       it. It nearly went unseen: the first test seemed to go well because
       the table that survived had a primary key and rejected the repeated
       INSERTs. With a table WITHOUT a unique constraint, three rows
       turned into six.

    2. But `pg_dumpall --clean` emits `DROP DATABASE` and `DROP ROLE`, and
       the DROP ROLE of the role you are connected with ALWAYS FAILS. With
       ON_ERROR_STOP=1 that aborts the restore RIGHT AFTER having dropped
       the database: the database disappears and is not recreated. It
       happened, live, and on top of that the command reported success.

    The correct way is never to drop databases: `pg_dumpall
    --globals-only` for the roles (which a normal `pg_dump` loses) and one
    `pg_dump --clean --if-exists` per database, which deletes and recreates
    the OBJECTS inside without touching the container.
    """
    pod, ns = b["pod"], b["ns"]
    v = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "postgres", "--version"])

    g = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 'pg_dumpall -U "$POSTGRES_USER" --globals-only'], binary=True)
    if g.returncode != 0 or not g.stdout:
        die(f"pg_dumpall --globals-only failed over {ns}/{pod}: "
            f"{(g.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
    fg = f"{b['org']}-{b['servicio']}-globals.sql"
    open(os.path.join(target, fg), "wb").write(g.stdout)

    databases = []
    for database in databases_of(ns, pod):
        d = kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                     f'pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" --clean --if-exists -d {database}'],
                    binary=True)
        if d.returncode != 0:
            die(f"the pg_dump of {database} failed: "
                f"{(d.stderr or b'').decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
        fn = f"{b['org']}-{b['servicio']}-{database}.sql"
        open(os.path.join(target, fn), "wb").write(d.stdout)
        databases.append({"nombre": database, "archivo": fn, "bytes": len(d.stdout),
                          "sha256": hashlib.sha256(d.stdout).hexdigest(),
                          "tablas": inventory(ns, pod, database)})

    # The keys of this dictionary are the bundle's MANIFEST, that is, the
    # on-disk format that `list` and `restore` read out of `.age` files
    # already written. They stay as they are: translating them would make
    # every existing bundle unreadable, which is a coordinated change and
    # not a rename.
    return {
        "tipo": "postgres", "org": b["org"], "servicio": b["servicio"], "ns": ns,
        "motor": (v.stdout or "").strip(),
        "globals": {"archivo": fg, "bytes": len(g.stdout),
                    "sha256": hashlib.sha256(g.stdout).hexdigest()},
        "bases": databases,
        "bytes": len(g.stdout) + sum(x["bytes"] for x in databases),
        "credencial_sha256": credential_fingerprint(ns, b["secreto"]),
        "secreto": b["secreto"],
    }


def capture_bucket(bk, target):
    ak, sk = s3_credential(bk["ns"])
    obj_dir = os.path.join(target, f"{bk['org']}-bucket")
    os.makedirs(obj_dir, exist_ok=True)
    objects = []
    with Tunnel("garage-system", "garage", 3900) as t:
        for listed in bucket_listing(t.host, bk["bucket"], ak, sk):
            key = listed["clave"]
            st, data, _ = s3("GET", t.host, f"/{bk['bucket']}/{key}", {}, ak, sk)
            if st != 200:
                die(f"downloading {key} gave HTTP {st}")
            # The key becomes a FLAT name: a key with slashes is a
            # directory tree the tar would have to reproduce, and the
            # restore reads the key from the manifest anyway.
            safe = key.replace("/", "%2F")
            open(os.path.join(obj_dir, safe), "wb").write(data)
            objects.append({"clave": key, "archivo": safe,
                            "bytes": len(data),
                            "sha256": hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest()})
    return {"tipo": "bucket", "org": bk["org"], "bucket": bk["bucket"],
            "directorio": f"{bk['org']}-bucket", "objetos": objects,
            "total_bytes": sum(o["bytes"] for o in objects)}


def bundle_age(p):
    return (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(p)) / 3600.0


def capture(root):
    """One bundle per organization, each one in its folder.

    The walk comes out of `sources()`, which already crosses the contracts
    with the cluster and refuses to back up if they disagree. Here it is
    only grouped: one organization = one folder = one bundle.
    """
    root = prepare_root(root)
    print(f"{gray}sources (contracts ∩ cluster):{reset}")
    databases, buckets = sources()
    for b in databases:
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} postgres  {b['ns']}/{b['servicio']}")
    for b in buckets:
        print(f"  {gray}·{reset} bucket    {b['bucket']}  {gray}(shared garage){reset}")
    if not databases and not buckets:
        die("no contract declares data to back up")

    per_org = {}
    for b in databases:
        per_org.setdefault(b["org"], ([], []))[0].append(b)
    for bk in buckets:
        per_org.setdefault(bk["org"], ([], []))[1].append(bk)

    stamp = time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S")
    rc = 0
    done_orgs = []
    for org in sorted(per_org):
        ob, obk = per_org[org]
        folder = os.path.join(root, f"org-{org}")
        os.makedirs(folder, exist_ok=True)
        # The name repeats the organization even though the folder already
        # says it: a bundle that travels alone —copied to another disk,
        # attached to a ticket— has to be able to say what it is without
        # its context.
        #
        # And the name is a CONTRACT with aegis check, which looks for
        # exactly this pattern to tell whether an organization has a
        # backup: it is not translated.
        out = os.path.join(folder, f"aegis-datos-org-{org}-{stamp}.age")
        print(f"\n{gray}── organization {org}{reset}")
        r = capture_org(org, ob, obk, out)
        rc |= r
        if r == 0:
            done_orgs.append(org)
    write_readme(root, sorted(per_org), done_orgs, stamp)
    return rc


def write_readme(root, expected, done_orgs, stamp):
    """The map of the backups tree, for whoever opens it a year from now.

    It lives in the ROOT of the destination and not in the repo, because
    the moment it is needed is precisely the one in which there is no
    repo: a disk plugged into another machine after losing this one.
    Safekeeping you do not know how to read is half safekeeping.

    And it is REWRITTEN on every capture instead of being written once,
    for the usual reason: a file drafted by hand ages and ends up
    describing a tree that no longer exists — with the particularity that
    here nobody was ever going to contrast it, because this directory is
    not versioned and no git check looks at it. Derived, it cannot lie for
    longer than it takes to get to the next run.

    It enumerates the organizations the CONTRACTS declare, not the folders
    that are there: a missing folder is exactly the finding, and listing
    the directory would hide it.

    The file is called LEER.md and stays that way: it is the on-disk name
    on every backup disk already in use, and renaming it would leave a
    stale twin beside the new one.
    """
    missing = [o for o in expected if o not in done_orgs]
    lines = [
        "# aegis backups — how this is put together",
        "",
        f"Last capture: {stamp} (the stamp of that run's bundles).",
        "THIS FILE IS WRITTEN BY `aegis data backup`. Do not edit it:",
        "the next capture overwrites it.",
        "",
        "## What is in each folder",
        "",
        "```",
        ".aegis-destino    the disk's mark. If its UUID does not match the",
        "                  filesystem this folder is on, the backups disk is",
        "                  NOT mounted and the capture refuses to write —",
        "                  without that the bundle would fall on the same",
        "                  disk it has to survive.",
        "platform/         the STATE: the store's secrets, the init's trail,",
        "                  the edge's tfstate. Written by aegis state backup.",
        "org-<name>/       the DATA of ONE organization: its databases and",
        "                  its objects. One bundle per organization, never",
        "                  shared.",
        "```",
        "",
        "**Both halves are needed.** With the state you raise an empty",
        "platform; with the data you have a dump nobody knows where to put.",
        "",
        "## The organizations, according to the contracts",
        "",
    ]
    for o in expected:
        if o in done_orgs:
            lines.append(f"- `org-{o}/` — backed up in this run.")
        else:
            lines.append(f"- `org-{o}/` — **WITHOUT a backup in this run**: its "
                         "capture failed. Whatever is in its folder is older.")
    if missing:
        lines += ["",
                  "> An organization that appears here and has no folder is one that",
                  "> was NEVER backed up. `aegis check` enumerates it against the",
                  "> contracts precisely so that that absence has a shape."]
    lines += [
        "",
        "## The encryption, and the only way to lose all of this",
        "",
        "Every `.age` is encrypted with the PUBLIC key of aegis's pair and opens",
        "with the private one: `~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key`, the same one SOPS",
        "uses for the repo's `.enc` files. One single pair for both things.",
        "",
        "**That key is NOT in any bundle**, on purpose and with a guard that",
        "aborts the capture if it appears inside. The chicken and the egg in the",
        "same basket protect nothing. What follows from that has to be said:",
        "",
        "> **If you lost the age key, everything on this disk is a brick.**",
        "> There is no recovery, neither partial nor by brute force.",
        "> The key goes to offline safekeeping, and that has to be a place",
        "> DIFFERENT from this disk — if they live together, one accident takes",
        "> both halves.",
        "",
        "The encryption is not ceremony: the `platform/` bundle carries the",
        "tunnel's tfstate with its token IN THE CLEAR. The bundle's `age` is the",
        "only thing protecting it.",
        "",
        "## How one is opened",
        "",
        "Without anything of aegis, which is the case this file exists for —",
        "this disk plugged into another machine, without a repo. `age` and",
        "`tar` are enough, and out comes SQL and plain files:",
        "",
        "```",
        "age -d -i ~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key <bundle>.age | tar -xzvf -",
        "```",
        "",
        "With the repo at hand there is convenience —it validates the manifest,",
        "compares credentials, restores against the right database—, but it is",
        "not a proprietary format:",
        "",
        "```",
        "aegis data list    org-<org>/aegis-datos-org-<org>-<ts>.age",
        "aegis data restore org-<org>/aegis-datos-org-<org>-<ts>.age --org <org>",
        "```",
        "",
        "`list` opens the bundle and says what it brings without restoring anything.",
        "",
        "Every bundle was verified as it was written: the tool decrypts it right",
        "after creating it and compares it byte by byte with what it captured. If",
        "one says NOT-VERIFIED, it was produced without the private key at hand.",
        "",
        "## legado/",
        "",
        "If it exists, those are bundles of the old format (before 2026-08-11)",
        "with ALL the organizations inside a single file. Its own LEER.md",
        "explains it. They are not moved into an `org-*` folder because they do",
        "not belong to one organization: counting them as one organization's",
        "backup would be a lie.",
        "",
    ]
    with open(os.path.join(root, "LEER.md"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write("\n".join(lines))
    ok(f"LEER.md up to date in {root} ({len(expected)} organization(s) according to the contracts)")


def capture_org(org, databases, buckets, out):
    stage = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-resp-")
    pay = os.path.join(stage, "payload"); os.makedirs(pay)
    try:
        pieces = []
        for b in databases:
            m = capture_database(b, pay); pieces.append(m)
            step(f"postgres {m['org']}/{m['servicio']}: {m['bytes']} bytes "
                 f"({m['motor']})")
        for bk in buckets:
            m = capture_bucket(bk, pay); pieces.append(m)
            step(f"bucket {m['bucket']}: {len(m['objetos'])} objects, "
                 f"{m['total_bytes']} bytes")

        man = {
            "version": 2,          # v2: one bundle = one organization
            "organizacion": org,
            "capturado": datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
                          .strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"),
            "instancia": subprocess.run(
                ["kubectl", "get", "ns", "kube-system", "-o", "jsonpath={.metadata.uid}"],
                capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip(),
            "piezas": pieces,
        }
        with open(os.path.join(pay, "MANIFIESTO.json"), "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump(man, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)

        # HARD GUARD, the same one as aegis state backup: the age key never
        # gets into the bundle. The chicken and the egg do not travel
        # together.
        for r_, _, fs in os.walk(pay):
            for x in fs:
                if x in ("aegis.key", "keys.txt") or x.endswith(".agekey"):
                    die("the payload contains age key material — ABORTED")

        tgz = os.path.join(stage, "bundle.tgz")
        with tarfile.open(tgz, "w:gz") as tf:
            tf.add(pay, arcname=".")

        pub = os.environ.get("AGE_PUBLIC") or ""
        if not pub:
            age_key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                                     os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
            if not os.path.exists(age_key):
                die("neither AGE_PUBLIC nor an age key to derive the recipient from")
            pub = subprocess.run(["age-keygen", "-y", age_key],
                                 capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(out) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        r = subprocess.run(["age", "-r", pub, "-o", out, tgz],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            die(f"age failed: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
        os.chmod(out, 0o600)
        ok(f"age-encrypted bundle → {out} ({os.path.getsize(out)} bytes)")

        # ── ROUNDTRIP. Without this it is "we have backups", not a proven
        # restore. It is really decrypted and compared byte by byte.
        age_key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                                 os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
        if not os.path.exists(age_key):
            print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} without the private age key: the bundle exists but was "
                  f"NOT verified (DR without proof of restore)")
            return 0
        check_dir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-ver-")
        try:
            d = subprocess.run(["age", "-d", "-i", age_key, out], capture_output=True)
            if d.returncode != 0:
                die("the bundle does not decrypt with the age key — do NOT trust it")
            with tarfile.open(fileobj=__import__("io").BytesIO(d.stdout), mode="r:gz") as tf:
                tf.extractall(check_dir, filter="data")
            bad_ones = []
            for p in pieces:
                if p["tipo"] == "postgres":
                    for piece in [p["globals"]] + p["bases"]:
                        q = os.path.join(check_dir, piece["archivo"])
                        if not os.path.exists(q) or \
                           hashlib.sha256(open(q, "rb").read()).hexdigest() != piece["sha256"]:
                            bad_ones.append(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']}:{piece['archivo']}")
                else:
                    for o in p["objetos"]:
                        q = os.path.join(check_dir, p["directorio"], o["archivo"])
                        if not os.path.exists(q) or \
                           hashlib.sha256(open(q, "rb").read()).hexdigest() != o["sha256"]:
                            bad_ones.append(f"{p['bucket']}:{o['clave']}")
            if bad_ones:
                die(f"ROUNDTRIP FAILED — it does not reproduce: {', '.join(bad_ones)}")
            ok(f"ROUNDTRIP OK — the bundle decrypts and reproduces the "
               f"{len(pieces)} pieces byte by byte")
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(check_dir, ignore_errors=True)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(stage, ignore_errors=True)

    sink = os.environ.get("AEGIS_BACKUP_SINK")
    if sink:
        r = subprocess.run(["bash", "-c", sink, "aegis-sink", out])
        if r.returncode != 0:
            die(f"the sink FAILED — the bundle is at {out} but it did not leave here")
        ok("sink OK — the bundle left the machine")
    else:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} without AEGIS_BACKUP_SINK: the bundle stays on the SAME "
              f"machine you want to survive")
    return 0


# ── reading and restoring ───────────────────────────────────────────
def open_bundle(bundle):
    age_key = os.environ.get("SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE",
                             os.path.expanduser("~/.config/sops/age/aegis.key"))
    if not os.path.exists(age_key):
        die(f"without the private age key ({age_key}) — it is the irreducible; without "
            f"it the bundle does not decrypt, and so it must be")
    d = subprocess.run(["age", "-d", "-i", age_key, bundle], capture_output=True)
    if d.returncode != 0:
        die(f"it does not decrypt: {d.stderr.decode(errors='replace')[:200]}")
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aegis-rest-")
    with tarfile.open(fileobj=__import__("io").BytesIO(d.stdout), mode="r:gz") as tf:
        tf.extractall(tmp, filter="data")
    man = json.load(open(os.path.join(tmp, "MANIFIESTO.json"), encoding="utf-8"))
    return tmp, man


def list_bundle(bundle):
    tmp, man = open_bundle(bundle)
    try:
        print(f"{gray}captured:{reset} {man['capturado']}   "
              f"{gray}instance:{reset} {man['instancia'][:12]}…")
        print(f"{gray}age:{reset} {bundle_age(bundle):.1f} h\n")
        for p in man["piezas"]:
            if p["tipo"] == "postgres":
                print(f"  postgres  {p['org']}/{p['servicio']:10} {p['bytes']:>9} bytes  "
                      f"{gray}{p['motor']}{reset}")
                for b_ in p["bases"]:
                    tt = b_["tablas"] or []
                    print(f"            {gray}database {b_['nombre']}: {b_['bytes']} B, "
                          f"{len(tt)} tables, {sum(x['filas'] for x in tt)} rows{reset}")
                print(f"            {gray}expected credential sha256:"
                      f"{(p.get('credencial_sha256') or '?')[:16]}…{reset}")
            else:
                print(f"  bucket    {p['bucket']:20} {len(p['objetos'])} objects, "
                      f"{p['total_bytes']} bytes")
                for o in p["objetos"][:5]:
                    print(f"            {gray}{o['clave']}  {o['bytes']} B{reset}")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    return 0


def scale_consumers_down(ns):
    """Scales to zero whatever may have the database open, and remembers to what.

    ArgoCD has selfHeal on these apps, so it is going to want to give the
    replicas back. The window is seconds and the restore is faster, but if
    one day it stops being so the symptom would be a DROP DATABASE that
    fails, not corrupted data — which is the correct way to fail.
    """
    r = kubectl(["get", "deploy", "-n", ns, "-o",
                 "jsonpath={range .items[*]}{.metadata.name} {.spec.replicas}{\"\\n\"}{end}"])
    previous = {}
    for l in (r.stdout or "").splitlines():
        if not l.strip():
            continue
        n, rep = l.split()
        previous[n] = int(rep)
    for n, rep in previous.items():
        if rep:
            kubectl(["scale", "deploy", n, "-n", ns, "--replicas=0"])
    if previous:
        step(f"consumers down: {', '.join(f'{n}({r})' for n, r in previous.items())}")
        for _ in range(30):
            q = kubectl(["get", "pods", "-n", ns, "-l", "!aegis.dev/component",
                         "--no-headers"])
            if not (q.stdout or "").strip():
                break
            time.sleep(2)
    return previous


def scale_consumers_up(ns, previous):
    for n, rep in previous.items():
        if rep:
            kubectl(["scale", "deploy", n, "-n", ns, f"--replicas={rep}"])
    if previous:
        step(f"consumers back up: "
             f"{', '.join(f'{n}({r})' for n, r in previous.items())}")


def cut_connections(ns, pod):
    """The sessions left over after bringing the pods down.

    A pod that is going away can leave the connection for a while, and ONE
    is enough for DROP DATABASE to fail.
    """
    kubectl(["exec", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
             'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d postgres -Atc '
             '"SELECT pg_terminate_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity '
             'WHERE pid <> pg_backend_pid() AND datname IS NOT NULL;"'])


def role_can_log_in(ns, pod, user, password, database):
    """Opens the database WITH the Secret's credential, the way the app does.

    Over the unix socket this would prove NOTHING: the image's pg_hba
    trusts local connections, so a wrong password gets in all the same and
    the test would be green by construction. 127.0.0.1 goes through the
    `host` rules, which are the ones the application meets — the same
    principle as everywhere else here: the defining test is the one the
    real consumer makes.

    The password reaches the container over stdin and is read by the shell
    that is already inside. It never appears in argv, not on the host's
    side and not in the pod's, which is where `/proc/PID/cmdline` is
    world-readable.
    """
    r = kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 "IFS= read -r PGPASSWORD; export PGPASSWORD; "
                 f"exec psql -h 127.0.0.1 -U {shq(user)} -d {shq(database)} "
                 f"-Atc {shq('SELECT 1')}"],
                binary=True, stdin_data=(password + "\n").encode())
    return r.returncode == 0 and (r.stdout or b"").strip() == b"1"


def realign_role(ns, pod, secret, database):
    """Puts the role's password back in step with the Secret of THIS instance.

    The globals dump carries the SCRAM hash each role had AT CAPTURE
    TIME, so loading it reinstalls the OLD password. Restoring on the
    machine that took the capture, that is exactly what you want.
    Restoring on a NEW one it is not: that instance generated its own
    credential, with its own key, and the dump knows nothing about it —
    the database ends up expecting one password and the pod presenting
    another. The symptom is an `authentication failed` in the
    application, which looks like a bug in the application.

    Measured in the rehearsal of 2026-08-27: the restore finished, said
    it was verified, and the organization did not come up until somebody
    typed an ALTER ROLE by hand. A step that is documented and not
    executed is a step that does not exist.

    The password never becomes an argument: the statement travels as
    psql's script, over stdin, the same road the dump takes.
    """
    user = secret_value(ns, secret, "usuario")
    password = secret_value(ns, secret, "password")
    if user is None or password is None:
        die(f"{ns}/{secret} does not carry `usuario` and `password`: the role "
            f"cannot be realigned, and a restore without realigning leaves the "
            f"application locked out of its own database")
    user = user.decode(errors="replace").strip()
    password = password.decode(errors="replace")
    if any(c in password for c in "\r\n\x00"):
        die(f"the password in {ns}/{secret} carries a line break: it cannot "
            f"travel over a line-oriented channel — and the container's "
            f"environment variable would not carry it either, so the "
            f"credential is already broken before this")

    sql = (f"ALTER ROLE {sql_identifier(user)} WITH LOGIN "
           f"PASSWORD {sql_literal(password)};\n")
    r = kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", ns, pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                 'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d postgres -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1'],
                binary=True, stdin_data=sql.encode())
    if r.returncode != 0:
        # Its output is NOT reproduced, and that is not laziness: on a
        # syntax error psql echoes the offending LINE, and that line is
        # the one carrying the credential.
        die(f"the ALTER ROLE over {ns}/{pod} failed (psql rc {r.returncode}). "
            f"Its output is not shown: the statement that failed carries the "
            f"credential")
    if not role_can_log_in(ns, pod, user, password, database):
        die(f"the ALTER ROLE reported no error and the credential of "
            f"{ns}/{secret} still does not open {database}. That the command "
            f"did not complain is not proof: the drill of 2026-08-09 reported "
            f"success over a database that had been deleted")
    return user


def restore_bucket(p, payload):
    """Puts the bundle's objects back into the destination's bucket.

    Until 2026-08-27 this half printed «not implemented» and stopped. The
    database came back and the bucket did not, which is the worst of the
    three possible states: the rows that came back point at objects that
    are no longer there, and nothing says so out loud — the application
    serves 404s over data the operator believes restored.

    Three decisions, with their reasons:

    · THE CREDENTIAL IS THE DESTINATION'S. The bundle carries objects,
      never keys, so the pair comes out of the live `garage` Secret of
      the organization's namespace. The namespace is not in the manifest
      either (the bucket piece never carried one): it is derived the way
      `sources()` derives it — one organization, one `org-<name>`.

    · AN OBJECT THAT IS ALREADY THERE IS OVERWRITTEN, unless it is
      IDENTICAL. Restoring means bringing the organization back to the
      captured state, and the database half already overwrites
      (`pg_dump --clean`); skipping the objects would leave the two
      halves at different dates, which is the very inconsistency this
      command exists to avoid. Identical is skipped by CONTENT and not
      by name —the ETag of a single-part upload is the md5 of the body—
      so re-running a restore is cheap and, above all, what the report
      says it wrote is what actually changed.

    · WHAT IS ONLY IN THE DESTINATION IS NOT DELETED, it is named. An
      object the bucket has and the bundle does not is later than the
      capture; deleting it would turn a restore into a loss no bundle
      can undo. Naming it is enough for whoever is restoring to decide.
    """
    ns = f"org-{p['org']}"
    bucket = p["bucket"]
    ak, sk = s3_credential(ns)
    written = identical = 0
    problems = []
    with Tunnel("garage-system", "garage", 3900) as t:
        for o in p["objetos"]:
            src = os.path.join(payload, p["directorio"], o["archivo"])
            if not os.path.exists(src):
                problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: it is not in the bundle's payload")
                continue
            data = open(src, "rb").read()
            # The same order as the database half: everything is checked
            # against the manifest BEFORE writing anything. A bundle that
            # does not reproduce itself does not get uploaded halfway.
            if hashlib.sha256(data).hexdigest() != o["sha256"]:
                die(f"{o['archivo']} does not match its sha256 from the manifest")
            # md5 for the ETag, which is an S3 identifier and not a
            # security claim — hence usedforsecurity=False, so it also
            # works where the library forbids md5 for hashing.
            etag = hashlib.md5(data, usedforsecurity=False).hexdigest()
            st, _, headers = s3("HEAD", t.host, f"/{bucket}/{o['clave']}", {}, ak, sk)
            if st == 200 and (headers.get("ETag") or "").strip('"') == etag:
                identical += 1
                step(f"object {o['clave']}: already identical ({len(data)} bytes)")
                continue
            st, body, _ = s3("PUT", t.host, f"/{bucket}/{o['clave']}", {}, ak, sk,
                             body=data)
            if st not in (200, 201):
                problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: PUT gave HTTP {st}: {body[:120]!r}")
                continue
            st, _, headers = s3("HEAD", t.host, f"/{bucket}/{o['clave']}", {}, ak, sk)
            got = int(headers.get("Content-Length", -1)) if st == 200 else -1
            if got != len(data):
                problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: written, and the bucket returns "
                                f"{got} bytes where {len(data)} were expected")
                continue
            written += 1
            step(f"object {o['clave']}: {len(data)} bytes written")
        # And the count, which is the part a per-object loop cannot give:
        # an object that never got as far as a PUT leaves no trace in the
        # loop, and the listing is where its absence has a shape.
        alive = {x["clave"]: x["bytes"] for x in bucket_listing(t.host, bucket, ak, sk)}

    for o in p["objetos"]:
        if o["clave"] not in alive:
            problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: it is NOT in the bucket after restoring")
        elif alive[o["clave"]] != o["bytes"]:
            problems.append(f"{o['clave']}: {alive[o['clave']]} bytes in the bucket, "
                            f"{o['bytes']} were expected")
    if problems:
        for x in problems[:12]:
            print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {x}")
        die(f"{p['org']}/{bucket}: the objects do NOT reproduce what was captured "
            f"— I do not call it good")
    later = sorted(set(alive) - {o["clave"] for o in p["objetos"]})
    if later:
        print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {len(later)} object(s) live in {bucket} and are not "
              f"in the bundle (later than the capture): "
              f"{', '.join(later[:5])} — they are NOT deleted")
    ok(f"{p['org']}/{bucket} restored and VERIFIED ({written} written, {identical} "
       f"already identical, {len(p['objetos'])} objects, "
       f"{sum(o['bytes'] for o in p['objetos'])} bytes)")


def restore(bundle, org, force):
    tmp, man = open_bundle(bundle)
    try:
        pieces = [p for p in man["piezas"] if p["org"] == org]
        if not pieces:
            die(f"the bundle has nothing of {org!r}. There is: "
                f"{', '.join(sorted({p['org'] for p in man['piezas']}))}")
        for p in pieces:
            if p["tipo"] != "postgres":
                restore_bucket(p, tmp)
                continue

            # THE GUARD THAT PREVENTS "I restored and it does not connect":
            # the dump brings the role's SCRAM hash, so the restored
            # database will again expect the password that was there AT
            # CAPTURE TIME. If the Secret rotated since then, restoring
            # leaves the database asking for one and the pod presenting
            # another, and the symptom —auth failed— does not look like the
            # cause.
            live = credential_fingerprint(p["ns"], p["secreto"])
            expected = p.get("credencial_sha256")
            if expected and live and live != expected:
                msg = (f"the credential of {p['ns']}/{p['secreto']} is NOT the one "
                       f"that was there at capture time.\n"
                       f"      Either it rotated, or this bundle comes from ANOTHER "
                       f"instance — and the second case\n"
                       f"      deserves a person, because it is not the same thing to "
                       f"restore a machine as to move\n"
                       f"      an organization into a new one. The role is realigned "
                       f"with the live Secret\n"
                       f"      once the dump is loaded, so what is missing here is the "
                       f"decision, not the repair.")
                if not force:
                    die(msg)
                print(f"  {yellow}!{reset} {msg}\n      {yellow}--force: I go on anyway{reset}")

            pod = f"{p['servicio']}-0"
            for piece in [p["globals"]] + p["bases"]:
                b_ = open(os.path.join(tmp, piece["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                if hashlib.sha256(b_).hexdigest() != piece["sha256"]:
                    die(f"{piece['archivo']} does not match its sha256 from the manifest")

            # ── THE PROTOCOL, which is more than a `psql <` ─────────
            #
            # The consumers are brought down because a pg_dump with
            # --clean drops objects the app may be using. And they are
            # brought back up NO MATTER WHAT: if the restore dies halfway,
            # leaving the organization at zero replicas would turn a failed
            # restore into an outage.
            consumers = scale_consumers_down(p["ns"])
            try:
                cut_connections(p["ns"], pod)

                # Globals WITHOUT ON_ERROR_STOP: the roles already exist
                # and saying so is expected noise, not a failure.
                g = open(os.path.join(tmp, p["globals"]["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", p["ns"], pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                         'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d postgres -v ON_ERROR_STOP=0'],
                        binary=True, stdin_data=g)
                step(f"roles restored ({len(g)} bytes)")

                for database in p["bases"]:
                    alive_dbs = databases_of(p["ns"], pod)
                    if database["nombre"] not in alive_dbs:
                        psql(p["ns"], pod, "postgres",
                             f'CREATE DATABASE "{database["nombre"]}"')
                        step(f"database {database['nombre']} did not exist: recreated")
                    sql = open(os.path.join(tmp, database["archivo"]), "rb").read()
                    r = kubectl(["exec", "-i", "-n", p["ns"], pod, "--", "sh", "-c",
                                 f'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d {database["nombre"]} '
                                 f'-v ON_ERROR_STOP=1'],
                                binary=True, stdin_data=sql)
                    err = (r.stderr or b"").decode(errors="replace")
                    serious = [l for l in err.splitlines() if "ERROR" in l]
                    if serious:
                        print(f"  {red}!{reset} psql over {database['nombre']}:")
                        for l in serious[:8]:
                            print(f"      {gray}{l}{reset}")
                    step(f"database {database['nombre']} applied ({len(sql)} bytes)")

                # ── THE ROLE, BACK IN STEP WITH THIS INSTANCE ───────
                #
                # Here, and not after bringing the consumers back up: the
                # pods return looking for the database, and finding it
                # with the password from the capture gives them a crash
                # loop whose message —authentication failed— points at the
                # application and not at the restore.
                #
                # It runs ALWAYS, including when the fingerprint matched:
                # setting the password the role already has costs one
                # statement, and the alternative is a branch that only
                # gets exercised on the day it matters.
                #
                # The database it proves the login against is the
                # organization's own (`POSTGRES_DB` is the organization's
                # name); if the bundle does not carry it, `postgres`,
                # which every server has.
                app_db = (p["org"] if any(b["nombre"] == p["org"] for b in p["bases"])
                          else "postgres")
                user = realign_role(p["ns"], pod, p["secreto"], app_db)
                step(f"role {user} realigned with {p['ns']}/{p['secreto']}: "
                     f"the live credential opens {app_db}")
            finally:
                scale_consumers_up(p["ns"], consumers)

            # ── VERIFICATION, the part that was missing ─────────────
            #
            # On 2026-08-09 a restore left the database DELETED and
            # reported success, because it looked at psql's exit code and
            # not at the result. Now the inventory is measured again and
            # compared against the one stored at capture time. If it does
            # not match, it is not restored, whatever psql says.
            problems = []
            alive_dbs = databases_of(p["ns"], pod)
            for database in p["bases"]:
                if database["nombre"] not in alive_dbs:
                    problems.append(f"the database {database['nombre']} DOES NOT EXIST")
                    continue
                now = {t["tabla"]: t["filas"] for t in (inventory(p["ns"], pod, database["nombre"]) or [])}
                for t in database["tablas"] or []:
                    if t["tabla"] not in now:
                        problems.append(f"{database['nombre']}: the table {t['tabla']} is missing")
                    elif now[t["tabla"]] != t["filas"]:
                        problems.append(
                            f"{database['nombre']}.{t['tabla']}: {now[t['tabla']]} rows, "
                            f"{t['filas']} were expected")
            if problems:
                for x in problems[:12]:
                    print(f"  {red}✗{reset} {x}")
                die(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']}: the restore does NOT reproduce what "
                    f"was captured — I do not call it good")
            n_t = sum(len(b_["tablas"] or []) for b_ in p["bases"])
            ok(f"{p['org']}/{p['servicio']} restored and VERIFIED "
               f"({len(p['bases'])} databases, {n_t} tables, the rows match)")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    return 0


def main():
    # Rule 1: verbs, not flags. And the same pair of names as
    # `aegis state`, because they are the same operation over another
    # thing: `state` is the MACHINE, `data` is the tenants' DATA.
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=cli.cmd("data"))
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="verb", required=True)
    s = sub.add_parser("backup", help=f"one bundle per organization (default: {DEFAULT_DEST})")
    s.add_argument("directory", nargs="?", default="", metavar="DIRECTORY")
    s = sub.add_parser("list", help="what is inside a bundle, without restoring")
    s.add_argument("bundle", metavar="BUNDLE.age")
    s = sub.add_parser("restore", help="restores the data of ONE organization")
    s.add_argument("bundle", metavar="BUNDLE.age")
    s.add_argument("--org", required=True,
                   help="restoring EVERYTHING in one go is not an operation worth making easy")
    s.add_argument("--force", action="store_true",
                    help="go on even if the credential rotated since the capture")
    a = ap.parse_args()
    a.capture_dir   = a.directory if a.verb == "backup" else None
    a.list_bundle   = a.bundle if a.verb == "list" else None
    a.restore_bundle = a.bundle if a.verb == "restore" else None
    if a.verb != "restore":
        a.org = None
        a.force = False

    if a.list_bundle:
        return list_bundle(a.list_bundle)
    if a.restore_bundle:
        return restore(a.restore_bundle, a.org, a.force)

    return capture(a.capture_dir or DEFAULT_DEST)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
